WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:03.470
In addition to testing your agents
skills and router capabilities,

2
00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.038
you need to ensure that your agent

3
00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:08.208
can respond to a user's query
in an efficient number of steps.

4
00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.844
In this lesson,
you'll learn how to assess this

5
00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:14.314
by computing a convergent score
and apply the test to your agent

6
00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:16.750
example. Let's go.

7
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.485
Now what is an agent trajectory?

8
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:21.221
A trajectory is simply the path
the agent takes

9
00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:24.424
through different router, tool,
and other logic steps

10
00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.226
for a given input.

11
00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:28.928
Looking at your example agent,
you can examine a few different

12
00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.031
trajectories
to get a sense of how these work.

13
00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:35.368
First, you can examine a query like:
"Which store had the most sales in 2021?"

14
00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:39.039
In this case, the agent would go from
the user to the router

15
00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:42.042
and then would go from that router to,
in this case,

16
00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:45.345
the lookup sales data tool,
as well as the data analysis tool.

17
00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.848
Back to the router
and then finally back to the user.

18
00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.985
Now one important thing to call out here
is that in this case,

19
00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:56.089
the agent has been built to allow for
multiple tool calls from one router step.

20
00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:59.526
So that's why you see both the Lookup
sales data tool and the data

21
00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:02.962
analysis tool being called together
in the one step between routers.

22
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.300
Some agents limit their routers
to only call one tool at a time.

23
00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.737
That's just an architectural decision that
you can make when building your agent.

24
00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:12.939
To look at a more complicated trajectory.

25
00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:16.309
You might have a query like plot sales
data volume over time.

26
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:22.177
In this case, your agent would go through
a user to the router to your to look up

27
00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:26.853
sales data and data analysis tools
back to the router, which then decides

28
00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:30.490
to call the data visualization tool
to generate that visualization code

29
00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:34.194
that would then return back to the router
and finally go back to the user.

30
00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.529
So these are pretty straightforward
trajectories so far.

31
00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:37.464
All told.

32
00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:40.467
But you can imagine that trajectories
get very complicated very quickly.

33
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.702
Your example agent has three tools.

34
00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:46.606
But agents in production can have ten, 20,
30 different tools that they use.

35
00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:50.710
And even some systems will start
to compose multiple agents together.

36
00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.680
So those agents can work together
with each other

37
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.515
to accomplish some sort of goal.

38
00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:57.150
In these multi-agent systems

39
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.186
you can have trajectories getting
very complicated very, very quickly.

40
00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:03.890
Now you might be asking
if your agent's output is correct,

41
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.726
does the trajectory really matter
all that much?

42
00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.729
And the answer is yes,
because efficiency matters in this case.

43
00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:13.299
Now, you might have a certain use case,
like if you're working on a hobby project

44
00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.368
or you're doing some research
where the efficiency

45
00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:17.770
of your agent doesn't matter.
That might be okay.

46
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.272
But in most production agents

47
00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:22.275
or real world
agents, some amount of efficiency matters.

48
00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:25.979
If you can answer a user's question
in six steps

49
00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:29.449
as opposed to 11 steps,
that means fewer LLM calls.

50
00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:30.750
That means less variability.

51
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.720
That means lower cost
and lower latency for the user.

52
00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:37.157
So how can you track and measure
trajectories?

53
00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.426
One way to do
that is using a tool called Convergence.

54
00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.229
Convergence as a measurement
for how closely your agent

55
00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.232
follows an optimal path for a given query.

56
00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:50.003
So you can think about this
as a measurement of how much your agent

57
00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.907
has converged towards an optimal path
for a certain type of query.

58
00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.242
So how do you test for convergence?

59
00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.978
Well, one way to do
it is using the following technique:

60
00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:01.915
You can run your agent
on a set of similar queries.

61
00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:04.684
In this case it could be
a bunch of questions that ask your agent

62
00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:09.684
to retrieve sales data from November 21,
and then construct

63
00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.293
a set of different graphs
or visualizations on that data.

64
00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:15.962
And the idea here is
you want questions that are similar enough

65
00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:18.965
where your agent really should take
the same path through each of them,

66
00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.367
but different enough
that there could be some variation

67
00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.636
that you can improve upon
within your agent.

68
00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:26.673
Next, you run each of these queries
through your agent and record

69
00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.709
the number of steps
taken for each of those queries,

70
00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:32.979
and then find the length
of the optimal path taken.

71
00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:35.648
In this case,
that's the minimum number of steps

72
00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.850
that your agent
took to get through one of these queries.

73
00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.687
Then you
can use all of those different numbers

74
00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:44.257
to calculate your convergence score,
which is a numeric representation

75
00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:47.260
of how often your agent
is taking that optimal path.

76
00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:51.197
Another way to think about the convergence
score is what percent of the time

77
00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:54.734
is your agent taking the optimal path
for that given set of inputs?

78
00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:58.538
So a convergence score of one means
that your agent's taking the optimal path

79
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:03.276
100% of the time, and your convergence
score will always be between 0 and 1.

80
00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:07.213
There's a couple things to keep in mind
when running convergence evaluations.

81
00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:12.452
First is that convergence evaluations
will typically not catch situations

82
00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.820
where your agent takes

83
00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.590
an unnecessary step every time
for every query in your test set.

84
00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:21.060
And the reason being here
is that the optimal path

85
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.095
in a convergence eval

86
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:25.098
is typically the minimum number of steps
the agent took to get through

87
00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.232
one of the queries.

88
00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:29.502
So again, if you have an unnecessary step
that's taken by all the runs of your

89
00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:32.672
agent, typically convergence evals
won't catch that situation.

90
00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:36.509
Second, you want to make sure
when running convergence evaluators

91
00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:40.513
that you're only running them
using four completed runs of your agent.

92
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:45.018
If your agent takes three steps
to get into a problem and then errors out,

93
00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.854
you don't want to track those three steps
because that will skew

94
00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:50.089
your convergence evaluation data.

95
00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.092
In this lesson, you've learned
what trajectory and convergence are,

96
00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.994
why they're important
to track and measure.

97
00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:57.997
And then one way to measure
and calculate convergence.

98
00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:01.301
In the next notebook, you'll implement
this convergence measurement

99
00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:02.368
and evaluation technique.

